        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped ions made coherent manipulation of individual quantum systems into a highly convincing route to quantum computation and simulation.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller provided foundational proposals, and Blatt made central experimental demonstrations; credit necessarily omits other major contributors.",
        "maturity": "The physics has been reproduced extensively and remains among the cleanest controllable quantum platforms.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Its proper boundary relative to optical-lattice simulation and quantum-information theory is contested."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B036",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael V. Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Geometric phase revealed a deep, measurable aspect of quantum evolution and has influenced phenomena across modern physics.",
        "attribution": "Berry and Aharonov made distinct foundational contributions to geometric and phase-related quantum effects; the conceptual lineage is wider than two names.",
        "maturity": "The effects are firmly established experimentally and have remained generative for decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The combined label joins related but not identical conceptual discoveries."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B021",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Browaeys",
        "Mark Saffman",
        "Mikhail D. Lukin"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Programmable Rydberg arrays offer exceptionally direct control of strongly interacting atoms and access to new quantum many-body regimes.",
        "attribution": "Browaeys and Lukin led decisive experimental architectures, while Saffman provided crucial Rydberg-quantum-information foundations.",
        "maturity": "Many laboratories now operate such arrays and important many-body results are reproducible.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field is comparatively young and its most lasting discoveries may not yet be settled."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
